WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.800
Kidnap-happy neighbors, tattoos that&nbsp;
everyone hates, and a finale leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.300
you wanting less. The smoke monster wasn't&nbsp;
the only thing that went wrong with "Lost."

00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:14.520
"Lost" suffered from its own groundbreaking&nbsp;
nature in a certain way. The model of network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.660
television has historically let series run&nbsp;
as long as viewers are still tuning in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.400
According to Collider, the creators of "Lost"&nbsp;
originally planned on just three seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.840
and then found themselves in a strange&nbsp;
reverse negotiation with the studio to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.320
actually let them plan on ending the&nbsp;
show. Until that deal was hashed out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.880
they had to stall for time and add more mystery&nbsp;
elements without revealing too much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.640
endgame too soon. ABC's initial offer was to&nbsp;
let the writers end "Lost" after 10 seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.220
so it's relatively fortunate that showrunner&nbsp;
Damon Lindelof negotiated them down to six.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.780
As a result, after a propulsive and&nbsp;
well-paced first season based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.560
mystery of what was in the hatch, the&nbsp;
middle seasons of "Lost" begin to feel a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.180
little spread thin. Lindelof in particular&nbsp;
took this lesson and applied it to future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.380
projects that he kept relatively short&nbsp;
and sweet, often to critical acclaim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.760
like the single season of "Watchmen" or&nbsp;
the three seasons of "The Leftovers."

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.800
For all the cliffhangers and inexplicable&nbsp;
polar bears, the main appeal of "Lost"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.940
is its cast of characters. It finds a&nbsp;
successful formula right off the bat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.260
with most episodes telling the story of&nbsp;
the survivors on the island intercut with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.820
flashbacks to one of their pre-crash lives,&nbsp;
often with dramatic parallels to what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.980
facing in the present. It was a very successful&nbsp;
formula… at least for the first few seasons.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:37.200
Arguably the flashbacks are all diminishing&nbsp;
returns after "Walkabout," where we learn that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.520
the character John Locke was formerly paralyzed,&nbsp;
but it's indisputable that the narrative device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.340
was worn out by the time we sit through an episode&nbsp;
dedicated to how Jack got his tattoos, an episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.680
that even Lindelof has noted in interviews as&nbsp;
one of the worst of the series. Especially as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.840
mystery in the present dragged on without answers,&nbsp;
these flashbacks came off as filler in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.240
meet the studio's demand for more episodes. The&nbsp;
flashbacks never technically stop, but beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.126
with Season 3's brilliant surprise flash-forwards,&nbsp;
they become less and less frequent.

00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:09.626
"We have to go back!"

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.360
"Lost" fans say that the show was always&nbsp;
more about its main characters rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.980
its plot and so shouldn't be judged harshly&nbsp;
for all of the holes and unresolved storylines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.600
it left behind. But that's denying the basic&nbsp;
reality that "Lost" had a TON of cliffhangers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:36.240
nearly every week, some new mysterious element is&nbsp;
introduced or explored, and the cut to the ominous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.620
title shot at the end of each episode interrupts&nbsp;
viewers desperate to see what's about to happen.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.420
Each season finale is perfect evidence&nbsp;
of this. "Lost" isn't structured in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.160
a way that makes any season particularly&nbsp;
satisfying as a story on its own; instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.140
each is designed to fold into the next, giving&nbsp;
us a trail of cliffhangers like breadcrumbs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.280
follow to the big series finale. If&nbsp;
"Lost" wanted to be about character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.540
it wouldn't have made us sit with so many&nbsp;
distracting questions between episodes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.380
seasons. It implicitly makes the show&nbsp;
dependent on the outcome of the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.240
and while it's eminently gripping, by design it&nbsp;
makes it harder to enjoy the journey to get there.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.460
The most memorable quote from "Lost" should&nbsp;
be one of its many important and gasp-inducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.720
lines. But because of the time it devotes&nbsp;
to one father's search for his missing son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.680
and then subsequent near-complete lack of&nbsp;
follow-up when the character disappears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.227
from the show, fans mostly remember one&nbsp;
line courtesy of the character of Michael.

00:03:30.227 --> 00:03:37.201
"WAALLLLLLTTTT!!! No! WALLLLLLLLLLLT!"

00:03:37.201 --> 00:03:38.220
Granted, the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:43.080
playing WALLLLLT, Malcolm David Kelley,&nbsp;
aged a whole year from season to season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.180
when only a few days would have passed on "Lost,"&nbsp;
so it's understandable that the writers needed him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.200
to fade from the narrative. On the other hand,&nbsp;
they probably should have considered that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.120
they made him such a key figure of concern in&nbsp;
the first few seasons, including them hinting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.502
that Walt had some kind of special abilities only&nbsp;
to never reveal what they were or how they worked.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.540
Much like Walt, the recurring string&nbsp;
of numbers holds significance mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.940
just because "Lost" says so. The show's&nbsp;
universe includes the sequence 4, 8, 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:19.320
16, 23, and 42 in so many different places&nbsp;
that it defies any sort of causal logic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.760
functions almost more as an in-joke for the&nbsp;
writing staff of the show, or a mildly fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.500
Easter Egg hunt for the most devoted fans. They&nbsp;
ultimately and most significantly correspond to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.700
the various candidates of the survivors to&nbsp;
replace Jacob as protector of the island.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.380
There are several strange steps between that&nbsp;
supposed origin and Hurley's encounter with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.420
them in a mental hospital, which leads him to win&nbsp;
the lottery and ultimately believe they're cursed.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:48.360
"4,8,15,16,23,42, 4, 8 - we're&nbsp;
dead - 15 - doomed and dead-"

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.560
"Hurley are you OK?"

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.760
"Huh? Yeah, awesome."

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.140
They're also the serial number on the hatch&nbsp;
and the numbers Desmond has to enter into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:00.960
the computer to keep something disastrous&nbsp;
from happening, and on and on and on. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.040
the time you start hearing about "The Lost&nbsp;
Experience" AR game and something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.940
"The Valenzetti Equation," it seems more&nbsp;
and more likely that the numbers are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.500
a connecting force between disparate parts of&nbsp;
"Lost," like a chaotic and tangled spider web.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.420
The Others are another great encapsulation&nbsp;
of what made "Lost" so frustrating for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.400
people. For two seasons, they're mysterious&nbsp;
bogeymen. They kidnap Claire and Walt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.260
and their leader, Ben Linus, is obviously&nbsp;
a dishonest and evil person. In Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.560
we learn that while they're a little&nbsp;
kidnap-happy, they're not entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.000
evil. They're normal people trying to protect the&nbsp;
island, and the real threat is from the outside.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.260
For the rest of the show, they gradually&nbsp;
become indistinguishable in purpose and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.100
practice from the Oceanic survivors,&nbsp;
even though the drama of the show thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.700
far has been thinking of them as, well,&nbsp;
"the others." By the time you're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.360
to understand the rivalry between Ben&nbsp;
Linus and the wealthy Charles Widmore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.040
you get lost in how the Others fit into the&nbsp;
story in the first place. The Others get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.668
new leader in the end, but don't really factor&nbsp;
into the endgame of "Lost" in a significant way.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.920
"Across the Sea," the second to the last&nbsp;
episode of "Lost," should have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.840
triumphant moment in storytelling. After the&nbsp;
better part of six seasons, answers about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.700
larger conflict happening outside the survivors'&nbsp;
comprehension are finally revealed. But almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.900
as much as the finale, the episode was met&nbsp;
with confusion from a viewing public ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.120
for answers. The whole story of "Lost" is a&nbsp;
quasi-biblical story about brothers in eternal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:29.520
supernatural conflict? And the Island is little&nbsp;
more than a fairy-tale backdrop for this story?

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.620
The episode lays bare something fundamental&nbsp;
about the calculus of "Lost." The show spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:39.000
six years puzzling our minds with questions&nbsp;
that presumably had rational explanations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.800
But in the end, the vast majority of plot&nbsp;
elements are a result of supernatural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:49.440
irrational powers and actions by two characters&nbsp;
whom we barely even know. The reliance on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.760
deepening mystery that supposedly takes place&nbsp;
in a universe like our own turns out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.440
a bait-and-switch for a larger symbolic&nbsp;
conflict. As preparation for the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.639
"Across the Sea" does its job by softening the&nbsp;
blow, but it's still a shocking and abrupt turn.

00:07:04.026 --> 00:07:08.820
There's truly no greater mascot for "Lost" and&nbsp;
its spirit of mystery than the smoke monster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:14.040
Initially, it's pretty accurately named, as it&nbsp;
appears to be a living cloud of black smoke that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:19.800
kills Oceanic 815's pilot and knocks over trees&nbsp;
menacingly. But soon, it starts appearing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:24.600
the characters' dead relatives, so it appears to&nbsp;
have different forms and properties. Even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.834
coming face to face with it, the character&nbsp;
John Locke was vague about what he saw.

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:34.380
"And what I saw…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.220
was beautiful."

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.780
Eventually we learn that it was The Man&nbsp;
in Black all along. The evil brother was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:45.060
locked in conflict for centuries with the equally&nbsp;
mysterious Jacob and had been stuck in smoke form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:49.200
since an encounter with the bright light known&nbsp;
as "the Heart of the Island." The point being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.180
The Man in Black aka the Smoke Monster was&nbsp;
less of a character or villain than a concept.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:59.700
Whatever it ultimately was, it even left many&nbsp;
experienced "Lost" viewers scratching their heads.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.640
We'll get to the specific nature of the flash&nbsp;
sideways in Season 6, but "Lost" is supremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.920
frustrating in that it saves the big reveal&nbsp;
until the series finale. Season 5 ends on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:14.940
huge cliffhanger, as Juliet detonates a&nbsp;
hydrogen bomb meant to sink the Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.520
and alter time itself so that none of the events&nbsp;
of the show ever happened. As Season 6 unfolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:25.020
it seems very plausible that they're depicting&nbsp;
that alternate timeline, as it depicts all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.380
major characters living different lives that&nbsp;
have little to nothing to do with the Island.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:33.360
Of course, that's far from what they turned out&nbsp;
to be. So "Lost" essentially revises the key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.640
to one of its biggest cliffhangers, and in the&nbsp;
process completely invalidates the sacrifice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.020
one of its best characters. It also raises&nbsp;
a lot of questions about how the Island is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:47.820
even habitable if a hydrogen bomb went off in&nbsp;
the '70s and the timeline remained the same.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:53.520
For a couple of seasons, leading man Jack and&nbsp;
leading lady Kate seemed destined to fall into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.560
the endless list of fictional 'will they or won't&nbsp;
they' relationships. Fortunately, the scruffy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.640
wisecracking Sawyer swooped in to break up&nbsp;
the moon-eyes and get together with Kate.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.360
Later, it's Sawyer and Juliet that&nbsp;
have an actually pretty compelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.160
time-travel–bonded romance, but there's also&nbsp;
Kate and Jack on the fringes making everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.800
insecure about things. This love quadrilateral&nbsp;
even factors into the motivation to detonate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.480
the hydrogen bomb in season five, as&nbsp;
Jack is supposedly so heartbroken over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.201
losing Kate that he wants to literally blow&nbsp;
everything up for a shot at a second chance.

00:09:26.201 --> 00:09:28.380
"Well damn Doc, she's standing right on the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.440
side of those trees - you want&nbsp;
her back, just go and ask her."

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.000
"Nah it's too late for that."

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.860
In a show full of supernatural and mystical&nbsp;
elements, and great conversations revolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:42.935
around philosophical debate, Jack's reasoning just&nbsp;
seems out of touch with the rest of the series.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.780
The flash sideways scenes in Season 6 turn out&nbsp;
to be glimpses of the afterlife. Regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:53.460
the particulars, in retrospect, it seems like a&nbsp;
fundamental misstep for "Lost" to confirm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:58.500
an afterlife exists, because it cuts the basic&nbsp;
dramatic tension of every scene in the show off at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:03.600
the knees. "Lost" is at heart a tale of survival.&nbsp;
Part of its huge popularity is that it taps into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.160
a primal drive, focusing on people trapped&nbsp;
and trying to survive on a deserted island.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.900
But if there's a confirmed afterlife within&nbsp;
the world of "Lost," what drama is there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:16.500
re-watching it and worrying about the&nbsp;
characters being in danger? They'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.280
just be getting an earlier ticket to the chill&nbsp;
afterlife where Jack's dad explains everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:25.320
calmly and everyone smiles at each other.&nbsp;
The "Lost" afterlife isn't just unnecessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:29.334
but also robs viewers of the chance to&nbsp;
find meaning in the characters' deaths.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:34.140
Even if you're on board with the flash&nbsp;
sideways world as a strange New Age purgatory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:39.900
"Lost" handles it questionably in a finale that&nbsp;
intercuts mildly confusing action sequences with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:44.220
what just seems like the cast wrap party.&nbsp;
It becomes impossible to care about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.600
battle between Jack and The Man in Black aka&nbsp;
Zombie Locke once you get the reveal anyway.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.900
The explanation that everyone has&nbsp;
gathered in this place because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.140
knew the time they spent on the Island&nbsp;
together was special and significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.000
is overall unsatisfying. It feels like&nbsp;
"Lost" insisting on its own profundity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.000
which is in no way earned and makes an assumption&nbsp;
of goodwill on the part of the viewer. "Lost" had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.380
so many loose ends and rabbit holes that no&nbsp;
finale would please everyone, but "The End"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.940
really feels like it was specially crafted to&nbsp;
raise even more questions than it answered.

